However, the prompt is ambiguous in one respect: Should the sum be as close as possible to the budget, regardless of whether it is above or below, or should it not exceed the budget (i.e., only sums ≤ b)? Both interpretations are possible.

I'll provide both versions:

**Version 1: Closest sum (can be above or below budget):**
```js
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  let closestSum = null;
  let minDiff = Infinity;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {
      let sum = arr1[i] + arr2[j];
      let diff = Math.abs(b - sum);
      if (diff < minDiff) {
        minDiff = diff;
        closestSum = sum;
      }
      if (diff === 0) return sum; // exact match
    }
  }
  return closestSum;
}
```

**Version 2: Closest sum not exceeding budget:**
```js
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  let best = -1;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {
      let sum = arr1[i] + arr2[j];
      if (sum <= b && sum > best) {
        best = sum;
        if (best === b) return best;
      }
    }
  }
  return best;
}
```

