**My Answer:**

To implement a guided process framework for user registration in ASP.NET Core with a single-page UI, you can use a combination of ASP.NET Core MVC (or Razor Pages), HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The idea is to present all registration fields on one page, but only one field is enabled and highlighted at a time. The user is guided step-by-step, with contextual help shown for each field, and cannot interact with other fields until the current one is completed.

**Step-by-step Implementation:**

1. **Backend Setup (ASP.NET Core):**
   - Create a model for user registration (e.g., `UserRegistration` with properties like `FullName`, `Email`, `Password`).
   - Create a controller with actions to render the registration page and handle form submission.

2. **Frontend (Razor View or HTML):**
   - Layout all input fields in a single form.
   - Add a help/info area for each field.
   - Use CSS to highlight the active field and visually disable others.
   - Use JavaScript to control which field is enabled, show the relevant help text, and validate input before moving to the next field.

**Example:**

*Model:*
```csharp
public class UserRegistration
{
    public string FullName { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
    public string Password { get; set; }
}
```

*Controller:*
```csharp
public class RegistrationController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index() => View();

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Register(UserRegistration model)
    {
        if (ModelState.IsValid)
        {
            // Save user, etc.
            return RedirectToAction("Success");
        }
        return View("Index", model);
    }

    public IActionResult Success() => View();
}
```

*View (Index.cshtml):*
```html
@model UserRegistration
<form id="registrationForm" asp-action="Register" method="post">
    <div class="form-group" id="step-fullname">
        <label>Full Name</label>
        <input type="text" name="FullName" id="FullName" disabled />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-fullname">Enter your full legal name.</div>
        <button type="button" class="next-btn" data-next="step-email">Next</button>
    </div>
    <div class="form-group" id="step-email">
        <label>Email</label>
        <input type="email" name="Email" id="Email" disabled />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-email">Enter a valid email address.</div>
        <button type="button" class="next-btn" data-next="step-password">Next</button>
    </div>
    <div class="form-group" id="step-password">
        <label>Password</label>
        <input type="password" name="Password" id="Password" disabled />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-password">Choose a strong password.</div>
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</form>
<script>
    // JavaScript to control guided process
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
        let steps = ['step-fullname', 'step-email', 'step-password'];
        let current = 0;
        function activateStep(idx) {
            steps.forEach((id, i) => {
                let input = document.querySelector(`#${id} input`);
                if (i === idx) {
                    input.disabled = false;
                    input.focus();
                    document.getElementById(id).classList.add('active');
                } else {
                    input.disabled = true;
                    document.getElementById(id).classList.remove('active');
                }
            });
        }
        document.querySelectorAll('.next-btn').forEach(btn => {
            btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
                let parent = btn.closest('.form-group');
                let input = parent.querySelector('input');
                if (!input.value) {
                    alert('Please fill in this field.');
                    return;
                }
                current++;
                if (current < steps.length) activateStep(current);
            });
        });
        activateStep(0);
    });
</script>
